      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the radius of a circle centered at one of the foci of the ellipse, which is tangent to the ellipse and contained entirely within it.\n\n1. **Identify the parameters of the ellipse:**\n   - The length of the major axis is 10, so the semi-major axis \\(a\\) is:\n     \\[\n     a = \\frac{10}{2} = 5\n     \\]\n   - The length of the minor axis is 8, so the semi-minor axis \\(b\\) is:\n     \\[\n     b = \\frac{8}{2} = 4\n     \\]\n\n2. **Determine the distance from the center to each focus:**\n   - The distance from the center to each focus is given by \\(c\\), where \\(c\\) is found using the relationship:\n     \\[\n     c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\n     \\]\n     Substituting the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n     \\[\n     c = \\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, the foci of the ellipse are located at \\((\\pm 3, 0)\\).\n\n3. **Understand the condition for the circle:**\n   - A circle centered at one of the foci is tangent to the ellipse and contained entirely within it. This means the distance from the focus to the point of tangency on the ellipse is equal to the radius \\(r\\) of the circle.\n\n4. **Find the radius of the circle:**\n   - The distance from the focus to the point of tangency is the same as the length of the semi-major axis minus the distance from the center to the focus. Since the ellipse is symmetric and the circle is tangent to the ellipse from the inside, the radius \\(r\\) is the distance from the focus to the point of tangency, which is also the distance from the center to the vertex of the ellipse minus the distance from the center to the focus.\n   - The radius \\(r\\) is given by:\n     \\[\n     r = a - c = 5 - 3 = 2\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
