      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to understand the relationship between the ellipse and the circle. Let's start by recalling some important properties of ellipses and circles.\n\n1. **Ellipse Properties**:\n   - The major axis of the ellipse is 10, so the semi-major axis \\(a = \\frac{10}{2} = 5\\).\n   - The minor axis of the ellipse is 8, so the semi-minor axis \\(b = \\frac{8}{2} = 4\\).\n   - The distance from the center to each focus (denoted as \\(c\\)) can be found using the relationship \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\):\n     \\[\n     c = \\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, the foci of the ellipse are at \\((\\pm 3, 0)\\).\n\n2. **Circle Properties**:\n   - The circle is centered at one of the foci, say \\((3, 0)\\), and it is tangent to the ellipse with no part of the circle outside the ellipse.\n   - The distance from the center of the circle (focus) to any point on the ellipse must be equal to the semi-major axis length \\(a\\), which is 5.\n\n3. **Determine the Radius of the Circle**:\n   - The radius of the circle is the distance from the center of the circle \\((3, 0)\\) to a point on the ellipse, which is also the semi-major axis length \\(a = 5\\).\n   - Therefore, the radius of the circle is 5.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]",
